[
  {
    "title": "The Devil's Chaplain On Trial",
    "body": "Dylan opens on the price other people paid, walking through the printed record of the trial of the Reverend Robert Taylor for blasphemy, and the case of his publisher Richard Carlile, who in October of 1819 was found guilty of blasphemy and seditious libel, sentenced to three years in Dorchester jail and fined 1,500 pounds, which the hosts calculate at roughly 280,000 dollars today. When Carlile refused to pay, his Fleet Street premises were raided and his stock confiscated, yet his wife Jane kept The Republican in print until it outsold the Times. Their infidel home missionary tour of the colleges earned Taylor the nickname the devil's chaplain."
  },
  {
    "title": "Pamphlets Against The Hydra",
    "body": "The episode treats media as the real lever of history. Dylan notes that Thomas Paine's Common Sense, published January 10th, 1776 as a fifty page pamphlet, sold over 500,000 copies and turned opinion toward independence, though Paine never held office in the United States government. He reads Paine's line that his own mind is his own church, then Andrew Jackson's rebuke to the bank men as a den of vipers and thieves, arguing that America privatizes gains and socializes losses. He points to the Emergency Banking Relief Act and the altered Trading with the Enemy Act of 1917, and to speech prosecutions now in Australia and England."
  },
  {
    "title": "Aldebaran In The Bull's Forehead",
    "body": "Reading Taylor's lecture aloud, the hosts follow his claim that Aaron is a Taurus figure, the personified genius of Aldebaran in the bull's forehead, since Cohen was the term given to stars of the first magnitude and Aldebaran was the tutelary star of the Arabian tribe of Misa, mirroring the sacerdotal tribe of Levi. Psalm 45 supplies the oil of gladness, the myrrh, aloes and cassia of April blossom, and the Pleiades as daughters of Atlas standing about the queen star, with Milton's sky tinctured grain quoted alongside. Dylan argues Elohim should read as goddesses rather than God, and floats a hidden pun on leaves."
  },
  {
    "title": "The Sun Hiding In Jesus",
    "body": "Dylan calls the system punastic and reads a monogram he photographed on a church pediment in Florence, IHS, as YHS, the monogram of Bacchus, sitting inside a sunburst framed by King of Kings and Lord of Lords. He takes Kion as a dialectical termination of Kiron, which Plutarch called the Persian word for the sun, unpacks the Greek spelling of Jesus as yay son, and traces king to Molech and lord to Baal. He contends that Greek writers were addicted to fables, cites Strabo, describes Greek mythological imperialism learned from his friend John McHugh, and notes that Homer means hostage."
  },
  {
    "title": "Miriam Answering At The Sea",
    "body": "The promised revelation lands in Exodus, where Miriam the prophetess takes a timbrel, the women follow with dances, and she sings that the horse and his rider are thrown into the sea. Taylor charges the English translation with Protestantizing Maria into Miriam, and Dylan adds that mem, resh, yod and final mem give Maria, since mare is Latin for sea and Maria is its plural. Chance calls that a kill shot. They tie mayim to Maya, mother of Hermes and Buddha, and to Myrrha, mother of Adonis, and Dylan maintains that both testaments were written in Latin before the Hebrew and Greek versions existed."
  }
]

